# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
# This project lives on an NTFS-3G mount. Killing `next start` while it holds
# build files leaves a `.fuse_hidden*` stub behind, which makes `rm -rf .next`
# fail with ENOTEMPTY and the *next* build silently corrupt — it reports
# success while writing a tree with no BUILD_ID and missing CSS. The recovery
# is to move the directory aside and rebuild; these are the leftovers of that.
/.next.old.*/
# Output of any `NEXT_DIST_DIR=.next-<something>` build — a verification or
# scratch build kept apart from `.next` so it cannot clobber a running dev
# server. The glob rather than one entry per name: every new dist dir
# otherwise shows up as 8,000 lint errors before anybody remembers why. See
# next.config.ts.
/.next-*/

# Reports written by `lhci autorun` when the audit is run locally.
/.lighthouseci/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# …except the template, which carries no secrets and is the only record of
# which variables the app needs. Ignoring it is how a new machine finds out
# the hard way.
!.env.example

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts
